# conversations-goodbyes.kb - Conversations / Goodbyes
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
#
# DO NOT add a concept called "goodbye" or "bye" here. The hand-written
# conv-farewell entry in data/conversation.json owns the bare word: a
# person typing "goodbye" is leaving, not asking a vocabulary question,
# and a knowledge entry with that concept outranks the behaviour entry.
# It was written, it cost one engine test, and the bot answered somebody
# who was waving with a dictionary definition.

parting | definition | A parting is the occasion of separating, and the word carries more finality than leaving does, which is why it appears in phrases rather than in ordinary speech. The useful contrast is between a parting that is expected and one that is sudden, since English marks the difference in the surrounding words rather than in the word itself. A parting of ways is a mutual and usually amicable separation, and the phrase survives from a much older sense of dividing property. | parting, separate, final, sudden, ways, divide | what does parting mean, parting of ways, is a parting final | parting | paghiwalay
send off | definition | To send somebody off is to see them leave at the start of a journey, and it is a public act with a ritual attached, which is why it is nearly always plural. Somebody staying behind sees them off; somebody going does not send themselves off. The word also means dismissing somebody harshly, and that second sense is common enough that the two are worth keeping apart in writing. | send off, journey, leave, ritual, public, dismiss | what does see someone off mean, is send off plural, send someone off meaning fired | send off | ipadala
exit line | definition | An exit line is the last thing said as a conversation ends, and it does more work than its size suggests, because the other person will remember it rather than the middle. The convention across cultures is to end warmly, but English also allows a joke at the very end, which is the most common way to signal that nothing has gone badly. What is avoided is a remark that needs a reply, since that does not end anything. | exit line, last, remember, warm, joke, reply | what is an exit line, how to end a conversation, what to say when leaving a party | exit line | panghuling pahayag
wrap up | definition | To wrap up means to finish something, and the word came from physical objects being tied up in a bundle, so it carries a sense of tidying as well as finishing. In a meeting it means formally closing the discussion rather than running out of time, and the two are not the same. The verb also works for eating a meal quickly, where the sense is finishing it off, and that use is common enough to be worth knowing. | wrap up, finish, tie, meeting, close, bundle | what does wrap up mean, how to end a meeting, wrap up meaning in a meal | wrap up | tapusin
remain in touch | definition | To remain in touch is to keep contact, and the word is the formal register of what is otherwise a very casual idea, being keep in touch. Remain adds a sense of continuing rather than resuming, which matters after a long gap. What makes the phrase social rather than practical is that it implies something will be done, and it is one of the promises people most often make and least often keep. | remain, touch, contact, resume, promise, keep | what does remain in touch mean, difference between stay in touch and keep in touch, is remain in touch formal | remain in touch | manatiling nakikita
